export function transfer(db, fromId, toId, amount) {
  // TODO: make this all-or-nothing, and notice when an account does not exist
  db.prepare('UPDATE accounts SET balance = balance - ? WHERE id = ?').run(amount, fromId);
  db.prepare('UPDATE accounts SET balance = balance + ? WHERE id = ?').run(amount, toId);
}

fund-js/24-sql-transactions-and-app-code.solution:
export function transfer(db, fromId, toId, amount) {
  if (!Number.isInteger(amount) || amount <= 0) throw new Error('amount must be a positive integer');
  db.exec('BEGIN IMMEDIATE');
  try {
    const debit = db.prepare('UPDATE accounts SET balance = balance - ? WHERE id = ?').run(amount, fromId);
    if (debit.changes !== 1) throw new Error('unknown source account');
    const credit = db.prepare('UPDATE accounts SET balance = balance + ? WHERE id = ?').run(amount, toId);
    if (credit.changes !== 1) throw new Error('unknown destination account');
    db.exec('COMMIT');
  } catch (err) {
    db.exec('ROLLBACK');
    throw err;
  }
}

fund-js/24-sql-transactions-and-app-code.explanation:
```js
db.exec('BEGIN IMMEDIATE');
try {
  const debit = db.prepare('UPDATE accounts SET balance = balance - ? WHERE id = ?').run(amount, fromId);
  if (debit.changes !== 1) throw new Error('unknown source account');
  const credit = db.prepare('UPDATE accounts SET balance = balance + ? WHERE id = ?').run(amount, toId);
  if (credit.changes !== 1) throw new Error('unknown destination account');
  db.exec('COMMIT');
} catch (err) {
  db.exec('ROLLBACK');
  throw err;
}
```

The transaction makes the pair atomic, the catch block rolls back whatever the failed attempt wrote, and the check constraint turns an overdraft into an error that takes the same path.

The plausible wrong answer wraps both updates in a transaction and stops there. It handles the overdraft, because the constraint throws. It does not handle a destination that does not exist: that update changes zero rows, throws nothing, and the commit makes the debit permanent. The trap test catches it. You have to read the changed-row count yourself.

A version without try/catch fails differently: the error escapes with the transaction still open, and the next `BEGIN` fails. The amount check matters as well: a negative amount is a transfer in the other direction that the caller was never authorised to make.

In Postgres the same shape applies. Most drivers offer a helper that takes a callback and commits or rolls back for you.

