fund-js/24-sql-transactions-and-app-code.solution:
export function updateTitle(db, id, title, expectedVersion) {
  const result = db
    .prepare('UPDATE docs SET title = ?, version = version + 1 WHERE id = ? AND version = ?')
    .run(title, id, expectedVersion);
  if (result.changes === 1) return { status: 'ok', version: expectedVersion + 1 };
  const row = db.prepare('SELECT version FROM docs WHERE id = ?').get(id);
  return row ? { status: 'conflict', version: row.version } : { status: 'not_found' };
}

fund-js/24-sql-transactions-and-app-code.explanation:
```js
const result = db
  .prepare('UPDATE docs SET title = ?, version = version + 1 WHERE id = ? AND version = ?')
  .run(title, id, expectedVersion);
if (result.changes === 1) return { status: 'ok', version: expectedVersion + 1 };
const row = db.prepare('SELECT version FROM docs WHERE id = ?').get(id);
return row ? { status: 'conflict', version: row.version } : { status: 'not_found' };
```

The version test and the write are one statement, so no other writer can get between them. The changed-row count is the verdict; the follow-up read only decides which kind of "no" to report.

The plausible wrong answer reads the row, compares `row.version` with `expectedVersion` in JavaScript and then updates by id alone. Every sequential test passes. The trap test lets a second writer commit right after that first read: the comparison was made against a version that is already gone, and the update overwrites the other writer's title. Keeping the version in the `WHERE` clause of the update is what makes the check hold.

An HTTP layer maps the three results to 200, 409 and 404. Postgres can return the new version from the update itself with `RETURNING version`; SQLite supports that clause too, from 3.35.

